WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.337 --> 00:00:04.475
(upbeat music)

00:00:04.475 --> 00:00:06.639
- [Voiceover] The birthday
paradox refers to the likelihood

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:08.776
of two people within a random group

00:00:08.776 --> 00:00:10.460
sharing the same birthday.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:13.861
Given the fact that there
are 366 possible birthdays,

00:00:13.861 --> 00:00:15.695
including February 29th,

00:00:15.695 --> 00:00:16.740
- 'Sup?
- [Voiceover] It doesn't

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.921
seem all that likely, but
in a room of 23 people,

00:00:19.921 --> 00:00:22.358
there's actually more
than a 50 percent chance

00:00:22.358 --> 00:00:25.529
that two of those people
share the same birth date.

00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:27.049
If that seems high, consider this,

00:00:27.049 --> 00:00:29.731
in a group of 23 people,
there are actually

00:00:29.731 --> 00:00:33.203
253 possible chances for a match.

00:00:33.203 --> 00:00:34.921
So, might not make sense to your brain,

00:00:34.921 --> 00:00:36.208
but that doesn't really matter,

00:00:36.208 --> 00:00:37.869
because, you know, math.

00:00:37.869 --> 00:00:39.030
So we're out here on the street

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.946
to put the birthday paradox to the test.

00:00:40.946 --> 00:00:45.288
By the time we hit 23 people
there's a 50.7 percent chance

00:00:45.288 --> 00:00:47.310
that two of them will
have the same birthday.

00:00:47.789 --> 00:00:49.995
Are we gonna be that lucky? I don't know.

00:00:49.995 --> 00:00:51.325
Let's find out.

00:00:51.654 --> 00:00:52.804
All right, so here's our first person,

00:00:52.804 --> 00:00:54.103
your name is?
- Bob.

00:00:54.103 --> 00:00:55.115
- Bob, when's your birthday?

00:00:55.115 --> 00:00:57.135
- January 30th, 1959.

00:00:57.135 --> 00:00:59.242
- Now since you're the first
person, there's no chance

00:00:59.242 --> 00:01:00.403
of you winning this.

00:01:00.403 --> 00:01:01.169
- [Bob] That's okay.

00:01:01.169 --> 00:01:02.411
- When's your birthday, Casey?

00:01:02.411 --> 00:01:03.657
- November 30th.

00:01:03.657 --> 00:01:04.573
- [Computer] No match.

00:01:04.573 --> 00:01:05.667
- November 26th.

00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:07.454
- [Computer] Ooo, no match.

00:01:07.454 --> 00:01:08.598
- October 15th.

00:01:08.598 --> 00:01:10.094
- [Computer] That's not a match.

00:01:10.094 --> 00:01:12.056
- Still don't have a match,
but it's pretty good.

00:01:12.056 --> 00:01:12.903
We're getting there.

00:01:12.903 --> 00:01:13.982
Hello there sir, what's your name?

00:01:13.982 --> 00:01:15.040
- Odin.
- [Computer] Cool name.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.339
- Odin, when's your birthday?

00:01:16.339 --> 00:01:18.639
- June 21st.
- [Computer] No way.

00:01:18.639 --> 00:01:21.077
- September 26th.
- [Computer] Not a match.

00:01:21.077 --> 00:01:23.479
- April 15th.
- [Computer] I don't think so.

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:26.381
- January 11th.
- [Computer] Not a match.

00:01:26.381 --> 00:01:28.634
- Here we go.
- [Lady] August 25th.

00:01:28.634 --> 00:01:29.853
- [Computer] No match.

00:01:29.853 --> 00:01:31.373
- [Voiceover] January 23rd.
- [Computer] No match.

00:01:31.373 --> 00:01:33.673
- September 18th.
- [Computer] No match.

00:01:33.673 --> 00:01:35.854
- Rod, when is your birthday?

00:01:35.854 --> 00:01:39.221
- My birthday is January 4th.

00:01:39.221 --> 00:01:40.697
- [Computer] Ooo, no match.

00:01:40.697 --> 00:01:42.051
- I'm so sorry Rod.

00:01:42.051 --> 00:01:43.783
- Well then I guess this is goodbye.

00:01:43.783 --> 00:01:45.907
- It is. Oh, oh!

00:01:45.907 --> 00:01:47.892
- September 24th.
- [2nd guy] October 31st.

00:01:47.892 --> 00:01:51.711
- May 8th.
- [Computer] Uh uh, nope, ooo!

00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:52.943
- None of these are matches.

00:01:52.943 --> 00:01:54.208
- Oh, shame.
- [Host] Sorry guys.

00:01:54.208 --> 00:01:55.542
- All right.
- [Host] I'm here with?

00:01:55.542 --> 00:01:57.726
- Jackie.
- [Guy] and Fernando.

00:01:57.726 --> 00:01:59.386
- And what do we got for birthdays?

00:01:59.386 --> 00:02:01.231
October 31st.

00:02:01.231 --> 00:02:03.403
- I think October 31st is a big winner.

00:02:03.403 --> 00:02:04.982
- [Lady] Yay!
- It think it's a winner.

00:02:04.982 --> 00:02:07.083
Let's double check, Jackie--

00:02:07.083 --> 00:02:08.848
- [Computer] That's a birthday match.

00:02:08.848 --> 00:02:09.823
- [Host] It's a winner!

00:02:09.823 --> 00:02:12.249
Spooky Halloween Bonus!
(eerie sound effects)

00:02:12.249 --> 00:02:13.908
- I got a special treat for you guys.

00:02:13.908 --> 00:02:16.326
- This is the best day ever. (laughing)

00:02:16.326 --> 00:02:18.431
- So there you go, there's
your nice Halloween cake.

00:02:18.431 --> 00:02:20.056
- [Lady] Thank you.
- Guys, thank you for,

00:02:20.056 --> 00:02:21.414
thank you for being born.

00:02:21.414 --> 00:02:22.308
So there you have it.

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:25.775
16 people and we found two wonderful souls

00:02:25.775 --> 00:02:28.591
with the same birthday,
on Halloween of all days.

00:02:28.591 --> 00:02:29.531
How spooky.

00:02:29.531 --> 00:02:32.718
So the birthday paradox,
it's a real thing.

